+This is the final feature-completion, integration and polish pass before release. The LE contracts below make every named requirement executable and testable.
+LE-01 — Scope, authority and execution
+Lunar Eclipse is the final feature-completion, integration and polish pass before release. Every feature named in this pass is shipping scope, not a suggestion or cosmetic-only task. It adds to the active specification; it does not excuse unfinished earlier requirements. The following product descriptions, reference images and LE contracts form one consolidated specification. Reference screenshots explain interactions, not another product's exact appearance.
+The coordinator MUST capture the latest canonical document revision, accepted repository baseline, active workstreams, supported platforms and existing data before implementation. It MUST maintain requirement-level VERIFIED, IMPLEMENTED-UNVERIFIED, MISSING and BLOCKED states tied to the exact revision/build, platform and evidence. Source presence, a successful build or a worker assertion is not verification.
+Use the existing ownership boundaries: Home owns shared runtime, packages, permissions and Mesh; Spaces owns conversations and sidebar/tab presentation; Dulche/Den retains its runtime and persistent AI-state responsibilities; Files owns canonical file identity and storage operations; Cards owns flashcard semantics; Planner owns planning entities. Existing shared CUI, Productivity Engine, Node Graph, preview/editor and integration services MUST be reused. Do not create parallel stores, permission brokers, installers or reduced embedded app implementations.
+The new mini sidebar and custom main sidebar explicitly replace the earlier fixed Spaces navigation hierarchy and its layout-specific acceptance test. Built-in Space behaviour, safe extension hooks and permission protections remain mandatory; required destinations MUST remain discoverable through the rail/switcher and Spaces browser. Customisation changes presentation, not security authority.
+In the older Home package paragraph, the bootstrap/outside-Home package client means the installer/bootstrap client, not the Launcher product. Launcher retains its Android/9to1-OS home-screen role and delegates package operations to Home/platform services. This clarification does not authorise a new app or a second package database.
+Droid remains post-release Android ROM work and Training Lab remains explicitly out of scope. Their headings do not add work to Lunar Eclipse. Do not inherit another pass's deadline, selected model, temporary Android limitation or production-release permission. An implementation pass is not permission to spend money, change production routing, publish publicly or remove production resources.
+Workers MAY resolve internal implementation details using existing contracts and the narrowest coherent design, documenting versioned interfaces and tests. The explicit defaults below are part of this specification. A genuinely new decision that changes product scope, security, compatibility or commercial policy MUST be recorded OPEN and block only dependent work; independent implementation MUST continue.
+Modularity
+Users will not need to install the entire 9to1 suite. They can choose individual modules/apps to install.
+Mandatory Modules include anything that is shared by multiple apps (dulche, cui, node editor, etc). These MUST be installed to use a 9to1 app.
+Home must also be installed.
+Every app other than Home is optional to select. Single-app frameworks required for startup or typical functions MUST install automatically with their app and cannot be removed independently while required. Other non-mandatory niche-feature modules may install on demand when their feature is requested. Shared modules remain mandatory under the rule above; installation does not require eagerly loading them.
+The apps shouldn’t be distributed as standalone msix installers. Instead, there should be a single installer that installs everything mandatory + the app you want.
+LE-02 — Package contract and lifecycle
+The native package manifest MUST classify each stable PackageID/version as mandatory shared core, optional app, app-required dependency or optional feature dependency. The mandatory-shared rule takes precedence when a component is both shared and niche. Mandatory means installed, not eagerly started or loaded into memory. Record dependency version ranges, platform/architecture support, integrity/signature metadata, activation requirements and reverse dependencies using Home's existing package service.
+The single trusted installer MUST install Home, all mandatory modules, the selected app and its app-required dependencies. Selecting a second app MUST reuse compatible shared packages. Internal platform package formats may be used; separate user-facing standalone MSIX installers for individual apps are not the distribution model. Native Windows and 9to1-OS use this contract through their supported packaging mechanisms. Browser apps use authenticated shared web services and MUST NOT require a local Home installation.
+Before a niche feature needs an absent optional module, show the required component, download/storage impact and Install/Cancel controls. A cancelled or failed install preserves the user's work and intended action; retry is explicit and safe. A required dependency cannot be uninstalled while an installed app depends on it. Uninstalling an optional app preserves user artifacts and dependencies required elsewhere. Updates, repair and rollback use staged validation and atomic activation; interruption MUST preserve or recover the previous valid installation.
+Opening an artifact or embedded app whose required app/module is absent MUST offer the same trusted install path, or an explicit unavailable state when installation is unsupported. It MUST NOT silently download unrelated apps, create a substitute viewer with lost capabilities, or claim a module is available solely because another Mesh device has it.
+Acceptance: clean-install one chosen app without unrelated optional apps; add another without duplicated core; decline and retry a feature-module install; reject removal of an in-use dependency; interrupt an update and recover; uninstall/reinstall without losing artifacts; verify Home version negotiation and the browser/no-local-Home case.
